{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Drink no wine nor strong drink, etc.</b> This command was juxtaposed here because wine was created only to comfort mourners (Eiruvin 65a), and Aaron might think that in his mourning, at least, it would be permitted to him to drink wine; therefore he was warned here that even in his mourning it is forbidden to him to drink it in a manner of intoxication. And Rashi explained concerning ״וַיִּדֹּם אַהֲרֹן״ that Aaron received reward for his silence — for this utterance was uniquely addressed to him alone. It is hinted here that anyone who cannot speak in the presence of the King is called drunk; therefore the utterance was uniquely addressed to him, to instruct him that he should not be so drunk that he cannot speak before the King, but should at all times be settled in his mind so as to be able to reply. This is what it says: ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳ אֶל אַהֲרֹן לֵאמֹר״ — with the word ״לֵאמֹר״ it sealed the entire matter, to tell you that he must always be ready to speak and to reply to the King, blessed be He. And this entire commandment came to him in the merit of ״וַיִּדֹּם אַהֲרֹן״." "Another matter: <b>It is hinted here to what our Sages of blessed memory said (ibid. 65a): wine enters, the secret goes out.</b> Therefore the utterance was uniquely addressed to him, like a king who reveals some secret to his servant and did not want others to know it. And He hinted to him that a person cannot enter the secret of YHWH except on this condition — that he not drink wine, for once wine enters, the secret goes out. Therefore the utterance was uniquely addressed to him, as one revealing to him alone some secret, and He warns him not to drink wine, so that he not reveal the secret." "Another matter: <b>For regarding silence one can have doubt —</b> whether he is silent and anguished, crying out in his heart against the judgments of YHWH, or whether he is silent and rejoices in his heart like the measure of the righteous, who rejoice in suffering. Therefore the utterance was uniquely addressed to him in order to reveal the purity of his thought — for the Holy One, blessed be He, rests His Shekhinah only from within joy. And this utterance — namely, that the Shekhinah rested upon him from the joy of his heart — through this it became known to all that he rejoiced in suffering like the measure of the righteous." "Another matter: <b>For this stillness</b> is an indication that he is unmoved by all the events that befall the body, and surely he is entirely intellectual, like one of the host of the height — for from the side of the intellect, a person is not moved by them. Therefore it is fitting that the utterance be uniquely addressed to him without an intermediary, for he too had become spiritual like Moses, on account of this stillness." "Another matter: <b>For the warning against drinking contains a hint toward silence,</b> for every drunkard multiplies words, as it is written (Proverbs 23:29–30): ״לְמִי שִׂיחַ… לַמְאַחֲרִים עַל הַיָּיִן״, and it is written (Zechariah 9:17): ״וְתִירוֹשׁ יְנוֹבֵב בְּתוּלוֹת״; therefore in the merit of ״וַיִּדֹּם אַהֲרֹן״ this utterance was uniquely addressed to him, for through it he would not multiply idle or harmful speech, for evil speech kills three (Vayikra Rabbah 5:1). Therefore it says ״וְלֹא תָמֻתוּ״ — apart from the fact that intoxication kills by nature, as it is written (Proverbs 14:12): ״יֵשׁ דֶּרֶךְ יָשָׁר לִפְנֵי אִישׁ וְאַחֲרִיתָהּ דַּרְכֵי מָוֶת״ — there is a way that is straight (יָשָׁר), the final letters of שֵׁכָר, for through שֵׁכָר everything seems in his eyes to be straight (לְמִישׁוֹר), as our Sages of blessed memory said (Yoma 75a): anyone who fixes his eyes on his cup — all the forbidden relations seem in his eyes to be straight (לְמִישׁוֹר), as it is said (Proverbs 23:31): ״יִתְהַלֵּךְ לְמֵישָׁרִים״. And this is what it says: ״וְאַחֲרִיתָהּ דַּרְכֵי מָוֶת״ — for the end of יֵשׁ דֶּרֶךְ יָשָׁר is the letters שֵׁכָר, and it is דַּרְכֵי מָוֶת, for it kills whether by way of punishment or by way of nature." "And He hinted in this passage when it is forbidden to drink it — that is what He said: <b>בְּבֹאֲכֶם אֶל אֹהֶל מוֹעֵד</b> ('when you come into the Tent of Meeting') — a hint that a drunkard is forbidden to come into the palace of the King, blessed be He, to pray before Him. And when is it permitted to drink it? When one sanctifies the holy Sabbath day at its entrance and at its departure, as our Rabbis of blessed memory taught (Pesachim 106a) from the verse ״זָכוֹר אֶת יוֹם הַשַּׁבָּת״ — 'remember it over wine,' etc. This is what it says: <b>וּלְהַבְדִּיל בֵּין הַקֹּדֶשׁ וּבֵין הַחֹל</b> ('and to distinguish between the holy and the profane') — at entrance and departure. And even this he must not drink in a manner of drunkenness to the point where all the impure [species of] creeping things are [indistinguishable as though] level before him; rather, he must drink in such a manner that he can distinguish between the impure and the pure, and in such a manner that he can instruct the children of Israel in all the statutes, and not err in his instruction."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The Talmudic citation '(עירובין סה)' uses the abbreviated form סה for page 65a; resolved as Eiruvin 65a.", 3 "דְּמִימָה ('stillness' / 'silence') here echoes וַיִּדֹּם אַהֲרֹן of the verse; both derive from the root דום.", 4 "The citation '(דב״ר ה י)' resolved as Vayikra Rabbah 5:1; the acronym דב״ר = דברים רבה is contextually impossible here since the quoted teaching 'evil speech kills three' is a well-known passage in Vayikra Rabbah."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:51.686310879Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}